{"t":1789136734,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"ff3e0618-252f-41fb-a601-8698b45433b9","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-09-11T13:04:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 111304\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n904 AM EDT Fri Sep 11 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nHeavier showers over the DC Metro have now pushed east.\nAdditional showers continue through early afternoon, though\nmostly from DC northward. A few thunderstorms possible in\nCentral VA late this afternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Storm chances linger through at least Saturday, with a\n  possibly drier finish to the weekend.\n\n- 2) Seasonably warm ahead of a mid-week cold front.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Storm chances linger through at least Saturday,\nwith a possibly drier finish to the weekend.\n\nMost of the heavy shower activity of the DC metro earlier this\nmorning has pushed east and out of the area. Additional light to\nmoderate showers are noted over northern MD, pushing east to the\nBaltimore area. A few light showers possible elsewhere, though\nit looks to be mostly dry into the afternoon. \n\nAdditional showers and a few thunderstorms develop this afternoon\nto evening over the Potomac Highlands into the central Virginia\nPiedmont region. Overall conditions today will yield more \nclouds than sun. This is accompanied by high temperatures in the\nmid 70s to low 80s, locally a bit cooler across the mountains. \n\nThe cold front initially north of the Mason-Dixon Line is\nforecast to settle toward the Virginia-North Carolina border by\ntonight. Despite the frontal passage, its more diffuse nature\nwill only bring a limited drop in temperatures and humidity\nlevels. Saturday morning will start off in the 60s which comes\nwith further shower and thunderstorm chances throughout the day.\nThe focus is driven by an elongated trough currently crossing\nthe Arklatex states. As this array of embedded shortwaves lift\nacross the Tennessee Valley toward the southern Mid-Atlantic,\nstorm chances increase in earnest. The 00Z high-resolution model\nsuite shows an uptick in rainfall amounts, some of which are on\nthe higher end (1 to 2 inches in 24 hours). With precipitable\nwater values in the 1.75 to 2.00 inch range and deep warm cloud\nlayers, some fairly efficient storms are possible in this\nenvironment. As such, the Weather Prediction Center supports a\nSlight risk for excessive rainfall on Saturday, especially\nacross the I-95 urban corridor. Will continue to monitor the\nsituation as new mesoscale guidance arrives.\n\nComing off temperatures similar to today, expect a slight warm\nup into Sunday with highs returning to the mid 80s. Some\nresidual shower activity is possible to start the day. However,\nthe local area will be in the wake of the passing shortwave\ntrough. Expect some increase in humidity as the boundary to the\nsouth returns northward as a warm front. Unlike previous days, a\nfair amount of sunshine is possible, particularly for the second\nhalf of the day. Sunday night's low temperatures will fall into\nthe 60s to low 70s.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Seasonably warm ahead of a mid-week cold front.\n\n500mb ridge builds Mon through Wed, before weakening as SW\ntroughing and associated sfc cold front move through midweek. \nSome spread on exactly what day this happens, but overall \nconsensus around Thu. This could bring showers and \nthunderstorms, but probability of svr wx remains low.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /12Z FRIDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nNow that most of the heavier rain has moved out of the area, VFR\nconditions prevail. A few thunderstorms are possible around CHO\nthis afternoon, where a PROB30 group is in the TAF. Overall \nwinds today will shift many times, but should remain around 5 \nknots or less.\n\nModels have shown the potential for lower ceilings late tonight\ninto Saturday morning. Some terminals could move over to MVFR \nat that point. Shower and thunderstorm chances increase on \nSaturday which will favor sub-VFR conditions at times, \nparticularly during the afternoon/evening hours. With the \nfrontal zone off to the south, prevailing winds will be out of \nthe east to southeast. Restrictions may persist into Saturday \nnight, while drier weather returns for Sunday amidst mainly west\nto northwesterly winds.\n\nVFR conditions Mon and Tue with light N'ly winds Mon \nveering E'ly Mon night and S'ly Tue.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nA brief northerly surge of wind along the cold front produced\nwind gusts around 20 knots over the open waters of the bay. That\nhas now mostly diminished, with winds around 10 knots. Winds \nturn more east to southeasterly by Saturday as the front pushes \noff to the south. This brings an increase in winds that may near\nadvisory criteria on Saturday. Afternoon/evening convection may\nalso support some hazardous marine conditions which would \ninclude lightning activity. Gradients weaken into Sunday with \ngusts in the 5 to 10 knot range.\n\nSCAs expected Mon with brisk N'ly winds. Winds veer out of \nE Mon night and then S Tue, but remain below SCA levels by Tue \nafternoon.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nTidal levels remain normal as anomalies sit near 0 feet early \nthis morning. Initial light winds will shift to northwesterly\nlater today which would support no coastal flooding issues. \nLooking towards late Saturday, water levels increase again \nahead of a warm front moving through the region. This should\npersist into Sunday before a cold front eventually crosses the\narea by Monday morning.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...KRR/BRO/CPB\nAVIATION...KRR/BRO/CPB\nMARINE...KRR/BRO/CPB\n"},"forecast":{"t":1789136700,"days":{"2026-09-11":{"hi":83,"lo":68,"pop":26,"short":"Chance Rain Showers then Mostl